And finally one of the most exciting moments, zapusk, zapusk, zapusk, zapusk.

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:14.720
That means launch, when the rocket blasts off from the ground into space.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:21.720
Repeat after me, zapusk, zapusk, zapusk, you just learned eight new space words.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:28.220
Cosmos, raketa, luna, звезда, земля, sputnik, uchoni, and zapusk.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.620
That's amazing, you're doing so well.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:36.180
Now let's look at a little bit of grammar, but don't worry, we'll keep it super simple

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:36.820
and friendly.

00:03:36.820 --> 00:03:39.580
In Russian every noun has a gender.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:43.960
There are three genders, masculine, feminine, and neuter.

00:03:44.680 --> 00:03:50.820
Most nouns that end with a consonant are masculine, so Cosmos, zapusk and sputnik are masculine.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:58.220
Most nouns that end with A or A are feminine, raketa, luna, and земля are feminine.

00:03:59.060 --> 00:04:01.760
Nutter nouns often end with O or A.

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:05.760
For example, the word pole field is neuter.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:09.300
In English we don't change words for gender, but in Russian we do.

00:04:09.720 --> 00:04:12.340
That's why we say bolshaya raketa.

00:04:13.040 --> 00:04:18.140
Big rocket, feminine, but bolshoy sputnik, big satellite, masculine.

00:04:18.840 --> 00:04:23.440
Listen carefully, bolshaya raketa, bolshaya raketa.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.340
Big rocket, bolshoy sputnik, bolshoy sputnik, big satellite.

00:04:29.900 --> 00:04:31.880
You don't need to memorize all the rules today.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.480
Just start noticing the pattern.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.360
Every time you hear A or O, at the beginning of an adjective, it's agreeing with the gender

00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:42.120
of the noun.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.720
You're already learning like a pro.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.600
Now it's time for our cultural corner.

00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:52.480
Russians are very proud of their space history, and for good reason.

00:04:53.220 --> 00:04:57.700
The first human being in space was the Russian cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:04.260
On April 12, 1961, he climbed into a small capsule on top of a rocket.

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:11.620
When the engines roared and the rocket took off, he famously said the word we love so much,

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:12.760
poyechali.

00:05:13.480 --> 00:05:16.940
That means let's go, or here we go.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:24.380
Even today, many Russian children still dream of becoming cosmonauty, which means cosmonauts.

00:05:24.380 --> 00:05:30.300
They read books about space, watch videos of launches, and imagine floating in zero gravity.

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.920
Space is a big part of Russian culture and identity.

