Then label everything with the Russian words we learned today.

00:06:35.760 --> 00:06:41.000
Raketa, Luna, Zemla, Zvizda, Cosmos.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:44.280
Point to each picture and say the word out loud.

00:06:44.280 --> 00:06:46.860
Your parents will be so impressed.

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:50.420
Now here's something really cool about one of today's words.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:53.540
Want to know a secret about Cosmos?

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:00.580
The Russian word Cosmos and the English word Cosmos are actually the same word.

00:07:01.220 --> 00:07:07.520
They both come from an ancient Greek word that meant order, beauty, and the universe.

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:12.560
The Greeks believe the universe was perfectly organized and beautiful.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:16.180
This word traveled through Latin into many languages.

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:19.820
It came into Russian in the 18th and 19th centuries.

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:26.240
The famous Russian scientist, Mikhail Lomonosov, helped bring many scientific words from Greek

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:27.980
and Latin into Russian.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:30.680
But here's the funniest part.

00:07:31.160 --> 00:07:35.860
The English word Cosmetic comes from exactly the same Greek root.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.680
The idea was that putting on makeup creates order and beauty on your face.

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:45.300
Just like the orderly, beautiful universe.

00:07:45.880 --> 00:07:54.300
So next time you hear the words Cosmos or even Cosmetic, remember they are cousins with the Russian word Cosmos.

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:58.600
That connection is your secret shortcut to remembering it forever.

00:07:59.500 --> 00:08:03.680
Молодец, that means well done or you're awesome.

